Task 1 review came back with two findings to fix. Fix both, re-run the covering tests, append a fix report to the same report file, and commit.

**FINDING 1 (Critical) — delete the two unused constants.**
`crates/paigasus-helikon-runtime-temporal/src/activity_input.rs:74-75`: remove `ACT_CALL_MODEL` and `ACT_INVOKE_TOOL`. Keep only `ACT_RENDER`.

Your read of the situation was right at the time, but the plan has since been amended (commit `eab63df`): Tasks 2 and 3 now each **define** their own activity-name constant rather than consuming one from Task 1. So leaving these here is worse than dead code — once Task 2 adds its own `const ACT_CALL_MODEL` to the same module you get a hard `E0428` duplicate-definition error. After this, `cargo clippy -p paigasus-helikon-runtime-temporal --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings` must be clean.

**FINDING 2 (Important) — the sentinel test doesn't prove what it claims.**
`decode_diagnostics_never_leak_payload_bytes` currently feeds a JSON **map** where a `String` is expected. serde_json renders that as `invalid type: map, expected a string` — `Unexpected::Map` never echoes nested content. So the test passes identically even if `decode_arg`'s `.map_err(|_| ...)` were changed to chain the real source error. It gives false confidence and would not catch a regression that reintroduces error chaining.

The leak path that *does* embed content verbatim is `Unexpected::Str`. Replace the test body with a single payload holding a bare JSON string, decoded against the 1-payload envelope arm (which expects a struct):

```rust
    /// Spec §5.1: the diagnostic lands in Temporal history and must never carry
    /// payload bytes.
    ///
    /// The payload is a bare JSON **string** decoded against the envelope arm,
    /// which expects a struct. That combination is the one that actually leaks:
    /// serde_json renders it as `invalid type: string "<content>", expected
    /// struct ...`, embedding the value verbatim. A map-vs-string mismatch would
    /// NOT prove anything here — `Unexpected::Map` never echoes nested content,
    /// so such a test passes whether or not `decode_arg` discards its source
    /// error.
    #[test]
    fn decode_diagnostics_never_leak_payload_bytes() {
        const SENTINEL: &str = "super-secret-tenant-token";
        with_ctx(|ctx| {
            let payload = ctx
                .converter
                .to_payload(ctx, &SENTINEL.to_owned())
                .expect("encode");
            let err = ctx
                .converter
                .from_payloads::<RenderInstructionsInput>(ctx, vec![payload])
                .expect_err("a bare string is not an envelope");

            let display = err.to_string();
            let debug = format!("{err:?}");
            assert!(
                !display.contains(SENTINEL),
                "Display leaked payload bytes: {display}"
            );
            assert!(
                !debug.contains(SENTINEL),
                "Debug leaked payload bytes: {debug}"
            );
        });
    }
```

Before you finalise: temporarily change `decode_arg`'s `.map_err(|_| ...)` to chain the source error, confirm the new test FAILS, then revert the chaining and confirm it PASSES. Report both observations — that is the evidence the test is load-bearing. Do not leave the chaining in.

**Then:**
- `cargo fmt --all`
- `cargo test -p paigasus-helikon-runtime-temporal --lib activity_input`
- `cargo clippy -p paigasus-helikon-runtime-temporal --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings`
- Commit with `fix(runtime-temporal): SMA-462 scope activity-name constants and harden the leak test`, staging only `crates/paigasus-helikon-runtime-temporal/src/activity_input.rs`.

Same constraints as before: no `git add -A`, no HEAD/branch-moving git commands, work synchronously in the foreground, don't bypass commit signing.

Return only: status, commit SHA, one-line test summary, the failed-then-passed observation for Finding 2, and any concerns.

