Good catch, and you were right to stop rather than pick unilaterally. The brief is wrong, not the code. I verified it: `crates/paigasus-helikon-core/src/model.rs:100-106` — `ModelRequest` has `messages: Vec<Item>`, `tools: Vec<ToolDef>`, `model_settings: ModelSettings`, none carrying `#[serde(default)]`. So `"request":{}` cannot deserialize, exactly as you found.

**Resolution — neither of your two options; a third that keeps the property the test exists for.**

The reason these fixtures must be literals is that a fixture built by serializing the current struct tracks the struct's drift and therefore asserts nothing. That constraint is about *what the test reads at runtime* — a `const &str`. It does not forbid you from working out the correct text during development.

So: serialize `ModelRequest::new()` once, look at the JSON it produces, and paste that text into the test as a hand-written `const &str`. The constant stays frozen — it will not follow future changes to `ModelRequest`, which is the whole point. Use a scratch print/`dbg!` to obtain it and delete that scratch before committing; do not leave any runtime serialization in the test.

Apply the corrected `"request": {...}` text to both failing fixtures — `call_model_decodes_frozen_envelope_literal` and `call_model_envelope_ignores_unknown_fields` — keeping `"added_in_a_later_release":42` in the latter.

**Add this doc comment above `call_model_decodes_frozen_envelope_literal`,** because a future reader will otherwise "fix" the test the wrong way when it eventually breaks:

```rust
    /// The `request` value is a frozen literal of `ModelRequest`'s wire shape as
    /// of this commit, NOT a value serialized at test time — a serialized
    /// fixture tracks the struct's drift and asserts nothing.
    ///
    /// If a future change to `paigasus-helikon-core`'s `ModelRequest` breaks this
    /// test, that is the test working as intended: `ModelRequest` is a nested
    /// core type, explicitly outside this module's field-evolution contract
    /// (rule 4), so a change to it IS a wire-compatibility break. Update the
    /// literal deliberately and treat the break as a release note, rather than
    /// regenerating the fixture to make the red go away.
```

Then finish the brief's remaining steps: `cargo fmt --all`, full `cargo test -p paigasus-helikon-runtime-temporal`, `cargo clippy -p paigasus-helikon-runtime-temporal --all-features --all-targets -- -D warnings` clean, and commit with the brief's message, staging only the three source paths.

